airline shares have been hammered since  the start of the crisis many companies  are struggling for their very survival  but those same shares have soared this  week on hopes of a recovery American  Airlines is up some 60% on the week  while International Airlines group which  owns ba was up 25% by this morning and  as you can see it is up further today  that's on hopes of air corridors across  Europe by AG shares some 12% to the good  right now alive now to British Airways  headquarters in West London where I can  speak exclusively to I AG's chief  executive Willie Walsh Willie welcome to  you you coming 12,000 jobs that seems  rather extreme well in what we're doing  is we're consulting with the trade  unions with the elected representatives  on potential job cuts no decision has  been taken in relation to actual  redundancies but what we have done is in  compliance with the law and it's  important to state this there is a legal  obligation on the company to submit  these documents to the elected  representatives and then engage in  consultations to give everybody an  opportunity to try and reduce to  mitigate to do anything we can to reduce  the impact of the potential  restructuring on the business that is  what we're doing at British Airways and  that is why we want the trade unions to  engage with the British Airways  management team to represent the people  that are working hard in British Airways  to ensure that the airline can have a  future  well the unions say that it's not  necessary to negotiate under these  section 188 redundancy notices they say  for example that Ryanair and easyJet  haven't had to resort to that well we  would disagree and in fact there's  plenty of legal precedence where trade  unions have sued and successfully sued  companies who have engaged in  discussions without submitting these 188  10 letters and in fact British Airways  was one of those companies sued by  United in the past and if you look like  this very recent case with monarch  Airlines who were sued by unite for not  going through the formal process so  we're acting under legal advice  having compliance with the law  and that's what we intend to do so you  know this is a really stressful time for  everybody but I think you've got to  realize and I'm sure people do we're not  flying last month  in in May we flew a total of four  hundred four hundred and eighty five  passenger flights 485 in a month we did  not buy lunch time on the 1st of May  last year so this is the most difficult  challenge the airline industry has ever  been faced it's unprecedented and we  need to take action to ensure that all  of the airlines in the book and  everybody in the industry is taking  action to ensure that we can survive  this and to ensure that we have a  competitive business for the future why  is the axe falling at British Airways  the most profitable part of IAG rather  than at other parts of the group well  what that is just untrue in all of the  airlines in the group and as you know  you mentioned it yourself Airlines  globally are pursuing restructuring you  know the focus here is on British  Airways because we're in the United  Kingdom but I can assure you that all of  the airlines in IAG face the same  challenge and the management teams at  all of the airlines are pursuing the  very same issues the scale of the change  is pretty similar for all of the  airlines and the measures that will be  taken I believe are going to be similar  but these are individual companies the  responsibility at British Airways is for  the management team at being at Iberia  will be the Iberia management team in  the same ways in Ireland it will be  their Lingus management team so it's  untrue to say that British Airways is  the only company in the group looking at  restructuring I can assure you all of  the airlines of the group are doing so  what about this allegation from the  Union of thuggish behavior that we heard  from Len McCluskey this notion that  you're going to make staff redundant and  then rehire them on lower salaries again  in I have to stress what we have done in  compliance with the law is to set out  proposals that we want to consult on  it's for the trade union to engage to do  the responsible thing to represent their  members who are crying  for the Union to do so to sit down with  the management team to try and come up  with solutions that is the responsible  thing to do it is by no means a exercise  that we enjoy doing I want to do it's an  exercise that we have to do we are  facing the greatest crisis the industry  has ever seen and we need to respond and  that means every management team has to  do what's right for the airlines that  they manage but some members the cabin  crew told us that they're facing pay  cuts of 70% I mean that seems pretty  severe I can tell you that the proposals  have been put forward with a view to  consultation we have not taken any  decision it would be irresponsible for  us to make these decisions without  giving people the opportunity to sit  down with us in consultation and come  forward with alternatives to what it is  we are looking to do now that's exactly  what the law requires us to do that is  exactly what we are committed to doing  and I can assure you the British Airways  management team have made themselves  available now for over 160 different  meetings with the trade union  representatives to try and progress this  issue I'm pleased to say we are in  consultation with the pilots union about  those consultations continue the reports  I get it are that some progress has been  made they have not concluded but I would  hope that the trade unions do what the  law requests of them because the  obligation is on us to consult you know  if they don't want to turn up to the  consultation clearly that's the decision  that they would take but I would urge  them to do what is right for the people  in British Airways and to sit down at  the table and engage in these  consultations so that we can together do  what is right I'm not involved in the  consultations it's the responsibility of  the British Airways management team they  are ready willing and able to do this  the problem is really a lot of staff  appear to have lost confidence in the ba  management and would probably rather see  you at the table  I think that's unfair in you know I  think the management team is working  very very hard to do what's right  they've demonstrated in the past that  they can do that British Airways is a  very well-run company the people in be a  from top to bottom right across the  organization are great people they all  work very hard they are all committed to  the success of the airline and I've  absolutely no doubt that everybody our  British Airways even don't stop  recognize that they may not have a  future in the business want to see  British Airways succeed what about this  campaign by unite few Toulouse lots at  Heathrow if you're losing 25 percent of  staff will you need as many slots in  future it's it is a challenge I will be  honest with you but don't forget this is  across the British Airways group so  British Airways as a significant  operation at London City at Gatwick and  at other airports so it's not just about  Heathrow but clearly we would like to  for the future secure all of the slots  that we currently have at Heathrow that  may not be possible it may may require  us to leave some of the slots that we  don't intend to operate but we would  like to see given that slots at Heathrow  tend to be at a premium we would like to  see if we can retain all of those if we  can't because the business can't afford  to do so there will be a regrettable  decision but it just puts into context  the crisis that we are facing today now  another point that the Union have made  to us is why is IAG going through with  the acquisition a very Roper that  doesn't look like the activity of a  cash-strapped business well first and  foremost I AG is not going through it's  the acquisition is done at an Iberia  level and I think it's important to  stress you know high AG is a single  economic entity with multiple operating  entities and when we talk about the cash  in the business we aggregate the cash  that is held by all of the individual  airlines  but if Liberia is to progress the deal  with Air Europa it will be because it's  in the best interest of Iberia and the  acquisition will be made by Iberia  not by British Airways British Airways  will not have any involvement in it  not a single penny of British Airways  money will go towards that acquisition  so each of the individual airlines have  cash balances they have their own  balloon receipts and it's the  responsibility of each of the management  teams to ensure that they're running the  business in an appropriate fashion so if  the Iberia  area Roper merger goes ahead it's not  going to in any way impact on the people  in British Airways  why hasn't ba thoughts taytay's in the  way that Lufthansa and air france-klm  have it's a great question but the  reality is state a does not be made  available in the UK so what British  Airways has done it has applied under  the CC FF the Bank of England scheme  where we've issued commercial paper that  the Bank of England have acquired we've  done so up to the maximum amount that's  available to British Airways which is  300 million pounds  we've availed of all other facilities  that we can in the UK but there is no  state guaranteed loan system in the UK  unlike other countries and there is no  appetite and you've heard the Chancellor  in many interviews make this clear you  know he doesn't have an appetite for  stepping in he insists that all  businesses must do what they can within  their own resources  first and foremost and it's only when  all of those resources and opportunity  and avenues have been existed exhausted  should companies go to look for state  aid so the system in the UK is  completely different in fact there is no  common system across Europe so we have  done everything that is within our power  to boost the cash balance of British  Airways we're exhausting every single  opportunity we can and if the UK  government like some other European  governments wants to make state  guaranteed loans available to business  and it gets sensible for us to do so I  can assure you we will apply for it now  obviously you were hoping to get more  flights airborne over the next few weeks  but the UK government's just introduced  this 14-day quarantine period for UK  arrivals how unhelpful was that  it's terrible I wrote to MPs last night  to say you know that this initiative has  in effect torpedoed our opportunity to  get flying in July we think it's  irrational  we think it's disproportionate and we  are giving consideration to a legal  challenge to this legislation  so we're reviewing that with the lawyers  later on today I suspect there are other  airlines who are doing so because it's  important to point out there was no  consultation with the industry prior to  enacting this legislation and we do  believe it is it can occur a rational  piece of legislation and briefly if you  would please Willie what progress you're  making on refunds for canceled flights  yeah we're doing everything possible and  I'm pleased to say that the staff who  are dealing with that are working flat  out to try and make sure that everybody  who's entitled to a refund gets it you  know I think we're well ahead of most  people in the industry and we will  continue to do so but we have committed  to our customers that if they're  entitled to a refund they will get done  refunds I really wash great talking to  you thanks very much free time good see  it okay thanks

